architecture.registrySchema:
## Tool Registry Pattern + Auto-Discovery

### The core problem
`import.meta.glob` is Vite-only. Next.js (webpack/turbopack) has `require.context` but it is unreliable under `output:'export'` for *type-safe lazy* aggregation, and it pulls everything into one chunk unless carefully tuned. The robust, framework-agnostic answer is **build-time codegen run via Bun**: a script scans `tools/**/tool.config.ts`, validates each, and emits a generated `registry.generated.ts` manifest that statically `import()`s each tool's component and wasm loader. This keeps code-splitting per-tool (each `() => import(...)` is its own chunk) AND requires zero edits to a central switch.

### `ToolMeta` type — `lib/registry/types.ts`
```ts
import type { ComponentType } from 'react';

export type ToolCategory =
  | 'text' | 'image' | 'data' | 'crypto' | 'convert' | 'dev';

/** Static, JSON-serializable metadata. Safe to import at build & for search index. */
export interface ToolMetaStatic {
  id: string;            // stable uuid-ish, never changes: "img-resize-v1"
  name: string;          // "Image Resizer"
  slug: string;          // url segment, unique: "image-resizer"
  description: string;   // 1-line, shown in cards + <meta>
  category: ToolCategory;
  tags: string[];        // ["png","jpeg","downscale"]
  keywords: string[];    // search-only synonyms ["shrink","compress dimensions"]
  icon: string;          // lucide icon name, resolved client-side: "ImageDown"
  relatedTools: string[];// slugs; validated against registry at build
  loadWasm?: boolean;    // hint: does this tool need a worker+wasm at all
}

/** Lazy runtime bindings. Each fn is its own code-split chunk. */
export interface ToolMetaRuntime {
  component: () => Promise<{ default: ComponentType }>;
  /** Optional: returns the per-tool wasm worker factory. */
  loadWorker?: () => Promise<{ default: () => Worker }>;
}

export type ToolMeta = ToolMetaStatic & ToolMetaRuntime;

/** What each tool folder default-exports from tool.config.ts */
export type ToolConfig = ToolMetaStatic & ToolMetaRuntime;
```

### Each tool self-registers — `tools/image/image-resizer/tool.config.ts`
```ts
import type { ToolConfig } from '@/lib/registry/types';

const config: ToolConfig = {
  id: 'img-resize-v1',
  name: 'Image Resizer',
  slug: 'image-resizer',
  description: 'Resize and re-encode images entirely in your browser.',
  category: 'image',
  tags: ['png', 'jpeg', 'webp', 'downscale'],
  keywords: ['shrink', 'compress dimensions', 'scale photo'],
  icon: 'ImageDown',
  relatedTools: ['image-converter'],
  loadWasm: true,
  component: () => import('./ui'),                 // ./ui.tsx -> default React component
  loadWorker: () => import('./worker.factory'),    // returns () => new Worker(...)
};

export default config;
```

### Codegen script — `scripts/gen-registry.ts` (run by Bun)
Uses Bun's `Glob` (built-in, no plugin) to find configs, emits a manifest with **static dynamic-imports** so webpack creates one chunk per tool. It also performs build-time validation (unique id/slug, valid `relatedTools`, valid category).

```ts
// bun run scripts/gen-registry.ts   (wired into package.json prebuild)
import { Glob } from 'bun';
import { resolve, relative, dirname } from 'node:path';

const ROOT = resolve(import.meta.dir, '..');
const glob = new Glob('tools/**/tool.config.ts');
const files: string[] = [];
for await (const f of glob.scan(ROOT)) files.push(f);
files.sort(); // deterministic output

// import each config at codegen time to validate it
const slugs = new Set<string>();
const ids = new Set<string>();
const entries: { importPath: string; varName: string }[] = [];

for (const [i, file] of files.entries()) {
  const mod = await import(resolve(ROOT, file));
  const c = mod.default;
  if (!c?.slug) throw new Error(`Missing slug in ${file}`);
  if (slugs.has(c.slug)) throw new Error(`Duplicate slug "${c.slug}" in ${file}`);
  if (ids.has(c.id)) throw new Error(`Duplicate id "${c.id}" in ${file}`);
  slugs.add(c.slug); ids.add(c.id);
  const dir = dirname(relative('lib/registry', file)); // path from generated file
  entries.push({ importPath: `../../${relative(ROOT, file).replace(/\.ts$/, '')}`, varName: `t${i}` });
}

// second pass: validate relatedTools after all slugs known
for (const file of files) {
  const c = (await import(resolve(ROOT, file))).default;
  for (const r of c.relatedTools) {
    if (!slugs.has(r)) throw new Error(`relatedTools "${r}" in ${file} not found`);
  }
}

const header = `// AUTO-GENERATED by scripts/gen-registry.ts. DO NOT EDIT.\n` +
  `import type { ToolConfig } from './types';\n`;
const imports = entries.map(e => `import ${e.varName} from '${e.importPath}';`).join('\n');
const arr = `export const tools: ToolConfig[] = [${entries.map(e => e.varName).join(', ')}];\n`;

await Bun.write(
  resolve(ROOT, 'lib/registry/registry.generated.ts'),
  `${header}\n${imports}\n\n${arr}`
);
console.log(`registry: ${entries.length} tools generated`);
```

### Public registry API — `lib/registry/index.ts`
```ts
import { tools } from './registry.generated';
import type { ToolConfig } from './types';

export const ALL_TOOLS: readonly ToolConfig[] = tools;

const bySlug = new Map(tools.map(t => [t.slug, t] as const));
export const getTool = (slug: string): ToolConfig | undefined => bySlug.get(slug);
export const getAllSlugs = (): string[] => tools.map(t => t.slug);

export const getRelated = (slug: string): ToolConfig[] =>
  (getTool(slug)?.relatedTools ?? []).map(s => bySlug.get(s)!).filter(Boolean);

/** Static-only projection for the searchable client index (no fn refs). */
export const SEARCH_INDEX = tools.map(({ component, loadWorker, ...meta }) => meta);
```

`registry.generated.ts` is gitignored and produced by `prebuild`/`predev` hooks, so adding a tool = drop a folder; nothing central changes.

architecture.workerInterface:
## TS <-> Rust Worker Interface

### Protocol types — `lib/worker/protocol.ts`
A discriminated-union request/response protocol with correlation `id`, progress streaming, transferables, and structured errors. Generic over each tool's op map so callers stay strict-typed.

```ts
/** Each tool defines an OpMap: opName -> {req payload, res payload}. */
export interface OpDef<Req = unknown, Res = unknown> { req: Req; res: Res }
export type OpMap = Record<string, OpDef>;

// ---- Main -> Worker ----
export interface WorkerRequest<K extends string = string, P = unknown> {
  kind: 'request';
  id: number;            // correlation id
  op: K;                 // operation name within the tool's OpMap
  payload: P;
  // ArrayBuffers/typed-array .buffers passed via the transfer list, not cloned
}

export interface WorkerCancel { kind: 'cancel'; id: number }

export type ToWorker<M extends OpMap> =
  | { [K in keyof M & string]: WorkerRequest<K, M[K]['req']> }[keyof M & string]
  | WorkerCancel;

// ---- Worker -> Main ----
export interface WorkerProgress {
  kind: 'progress';
  id: number;
  ratio: number;         // 0..1
  stage?: string;        // "decoding" | "encoding" ...
}
export interface WorkerSuccess<R = unknown> {
  kind: 'success';
  id: number;
  result: R;             // may contain transferable buffers
}
export interface WorkerError {
  kind: 'error';
  id: number;
  code: 'WASM_INIT' | 'INVALID_INPUT' | 'PANIC' | 'CANCELLED' | 'UNKNOWN';
  message: string;
  detail?: string;       // rust panic backtrace / context
}
export interface WorkerReady { kind: 'ready' }

export type FromWorker<M extends OpMap> =
  | WorkerReady | WorkerProgress | WorkerError
  | { [K in keyof M & string]: WorkerSuccess<M[K]['res']> }[keyof M & string];
```

### A tool's OpMap — `tools/image/image-resizer/ops.ts`
```ts
import type { OpMap } from '@/lib/worker/protocol';

export interface ResizeReq {
  bytes: ArrayBuffer;        // transferred
  width: number; height: number;
  format: 'png' | 'jpeg' | 'webp';
  quality: number;           // 0..100
}
export interface ResizeRes { bytes: ArrayBuffer; mime: string }

export type ResizerOps = {
  resize: { req: ResizeReq; res: ResizeRes };
};
```

### Worker entry (lazy wasm) — `tools/image/image-resizer/worker.ts`
WASM is loaded lazily *inside* the worker on first request, so the wasm chunk only downloads when the tool is opened (the worker itself is only constructed by `loadWorker`).

```ts
/// <reference lib="webworker" />
import type { ToWorker, FromWorker } from '@/lib/worker/protocol';
import type { ResizerOps } from './ops';

type In = ToWorker<ResizerOps>;
type Out = FromWorker<ResizerOps>;
const post = (msg: Out, transfer?: Transferable[]) =>
  (self as DedicatedWorkerGlobalScope).postMessage(msg, transfer ?? []);

const cancelled = new Set<number>();

// dynamic import => wasm js+.wasm become a separate chunk, fetched on demand
let wasmP: Promise<typeof import('@/rust/pkg/image_tools')> | null = null;
const getWasm = () => (wasmP ??= import('@/rust/pkg/image_tools').then(async (m) => {
  await m.default();           // wasm-bindgen init (fetches .wasm)
  return m;
}));

self.onmessage = async (e: MessageEvent<In>) => {
  const msg = e.data;
  if (msg.kind === 'cancel') { cancelled.add(msg.id); return; }
  if (msg.kind !== 'request') return;
  const { id, op, payload } = msg;
  try {
    const wasm = await getWasm();
    if (op === 'resize') {
      const onProgress = (ratio: number, stage: string) => {
        if (!cancelled.has(id)) post({ kind: 'progress', id, ratio, stage });
      };
      const out = wasm.resize_image(
        new Uint8Array(payload.bytes),
        payload.width, payload.height, payload.format, payload.quality,
        onProgress,                         // wasm-bindgen callback -> progress msgs
      );
      if (cancelled.has(id)) { cancelled.delete(id); post({ kind:'error', id, code:'CANCELLED', message:'cancelled' }); return; }
      const buf = out.buffer;               // Uint8Array -> ArrayBuffer
      post({ kind: 'success', id, result: { bytes: buf, mime: `image/${payload.format}` } }, [buf]);
    }
  } catch (err) {
    post({ kind: 'error', id, code: 'PANIC', message: String(err) });
  }
};
post({ kind: 'ready' });
```

### Worker factory (code-split target) — `tools/image/image-resizer/worker.factory.ts`
```ts
// `new URL(... import.meta.url)` is the webpack/turbopack-supported worker syntax,
// and is preserved under output:'export' as a hashed static asset.
export default function createWorker(): Worker {
  return new Worker(new URL('./worker.ts', import.meta.url), { type: 'module' });
}
```

### Typed client hook — `lib/worker/use-worker-op.ts`
Wraps any tool's worker with promise + progress semantics, manages correlation ids, transfer lists, cancellation, and worker lifecycle.

```ts
import { useCallback, useEffect, useRef, useState } from 'react';
import type { OpMap, ToWorker, FromWorker } from './protocol';

export function useWorkerOp<M extends OpMap>(factory: () => Worker) {
  const ref = useRef<Worker | null>(null);
  const seq = useRef(0);
  const pending = useRef(new Map<number, {
    resolve: (r: unknown) => void; reject: (e: Error) => void;
    onProgress?: (r: number, s?: string) => void;
  }>());
  const [ready, setReady] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    const w = factory(); ref.current = w;
    w.onmessage = (e: MessageEvent<FromWorker<M>>) => {
      const m = e.data;
      if (m.kind === 'ready') { setReady(true); return; }
      const p = 'id' in m ? pending.current.get(m.id) : undefined;
      if (!p) return;
      if (m.kind === 'progress') p.onProgress?.(m.ratio, m.stage);
      else if (m.kind === 'success') { p.resolve(m.result); pending.current.delete(m.id); }
      else if (m.kind === 'error') { p.reject(new Error(`${m.code}: ${m.message}`)); pending.current.delete(m.id); }
    };
    return () => { w.terminate(); ref.current = null; };
  }, []); // factory is stable per tool

  const run = useCallback(<K extends keyof M & string>(
    op: K, payload: M[K]['req'],
    opts?: { transfer?: Transferable[]; onProgress?: (r: number, s?: string) => void },
  ): Promise<M[K]['res']> => {
    const id = ++seq.current;
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      pending.current.set(id, { resolve: resolve as (r: unknown)=>void, reject, onProgress: opts?.onProgress });
      const req = { kind: 'request', id, op, payload } as ToWorker<M>;
      ref.current!.postMessage(req, opts?.transfer ?? []);
    });
  }, []);

  const cancel = useCallback((id: number) => ref.current?.postMessage({ kind: 'cancel', id }), []);
  return { ready, run, cancel };
}
```

### Parallel tools (`wasm-bindgen-rayon`)
For tools needing CPU parallelism the worker calls `await initThreadPool(navigator.hardwareConcurrency)` after `default()`. This requires the page be **cross-origin isolated** (COOP/COEP) — under static export there is no server to set headers, so the service worker injects them on navigations (see WASM boundary). Pool tools are flagged `loadWasm:true` and additionally documented to spawn nested workers; the host worker stays the single message endpoint.

architecture.sharedComponents:
## Shared Component API

All accept generic typing so a tool wires its own OpMap result type. Files in `components/shared/`.

### FileDropzone — `file-dropzone.tsx`
```ts
export interface FileDropzoneProps {
  accept?: string;                 // "image/*" — also passed to <input>
  multiple?: boolean;              // enables batch mode
  maxSizeBytes?: number;
  onFiles: (files: File[]) => void;
  disabled?: boolean;
  children?: React.ReactNode;      // custom prompt content
}
```
Handles drag/drop + click-to-browse + paste (clipboard images). Validates count/size/MIME, reports rejects via a toast. No upload — files stay in memory.

### ResultPreview — `result-preview.tsx`
```ts
export type PreviewKind = 'image' | 'text' | 'json' | 'binary' | 'diff';
export interface ResultPreviewProps {
  kind: PreviewKind;
  data: Blob | string | ArrayBuffer;
  mime?: string;
  filename?: string;               // for binary fallback display
}
```
Renders an `<img>` from an object URL (revoked on unmount), a monospace/`<pre>` block for text/json (with syntax highlight), or a "binary — N bytes" card otherwise.

### DownloadButton — `download-button.tsx`
```ts
export interface DownloadButtonProps {
  getData: () => Blob | ArrayBuffer | Promise<Blob | ArrayBuffer>;
  filename: string;
  mime?: string;
  variant?: ButtonProps['variant'];
}
```
Uses `lib/download.ts`: tries `showSaveFilePicker` (File System Access API) when available, else falls back to `URL.createObjectURL` + synthetic `<a download>`. Pure client, works offline.

### CopyButton — `copy-button.tsx`
```ts
export interface CopyButtonProps {
  getText: () => string | Promise<string>;
  label?: string;                  // default "Copy"
}
```
`navigator.clipboard.writeText` with a 1.5s "Copied" state; degrades to `execCommand('copy')` on insecure contexts.

### ProgressBar — `progress-bar.tsx`
```ts
export interface ProgressBarProps {
  ratio: number | null;            // null => indeterminate
  stage?: string;
  className?: string;
}
```
Wraps shadcn `Progress` (Radix). Driven directly by `WorkerProgress` messages: the tool's `run(op, payload, { onProgress: (r,s) => setProgress({r,s}) })`.

### BatchRunner — `batch-runner.tsx`
The orchestration primitive: takes N files, runs each through a worker op (via a pool), tracks per-item progress, and zips successes.
```ts
export interface BatchItem<R> {
  file: File; status: 'queued'|'running'|'done'|'error';
  ratio: number; result?: R; error?: string;
}
export interface BatchRunnerProps<M extends OpMap, K extends keyof M & string> {
  files: File[];
  factory: () => Worker;           // tool's worker.factory default export
  op: K;
  buildPayload: (file: File, buf: ArrayBuffer) => { payload: M[K]['req']; transfer: Transferable[] };
  toBlob: (res: M[K]['res']) => { blob: Blob; name: string };
  concurrency?: number;            // default navigator.hardwareConcurrency
  children: (items: BatchItem<M[K]['res']>[], zipAll: () => Promise<void>) => React.ReactNode;
}
```
Internally builds a `WorkerPool` (`lib/worker/worker-pool.ts`) of `concurrency` workers from `factory`, dispatches items round-robin, updates each `BatchItem.ratio` from progress messages, and `zipAll()` calls `lib/zip.ts`:
```ts
// lib/zip.ts — fflate, streaming, all in-browser
import { zip } from 'fflate';
export function zipFiles(files: { name: string; data: Uint8Array }[]): Promise<Blob> {
  return new Promise((res, rej) => {
    const record = Object.fromEntries(files.map(f => [f.name, f.data]));
    zip(record, { level: 6 }, (err, out) =>
      err ? rej(err) : res(new Blob([out], { type: 'application/zip' })));
  });
}
```

### ToolHost — `components/tool-host.tsx` (ties it together)
```ts
'use client';
export function ToolHost({ slug }: { slug: string }) {
  const tool = getTool(slug);
  if (!tool) return null;
  const Lazy = useMemo(() => lazy(tool.component), [slug]);
  return <Suspense fallback={<ToolSkeleton />}><Lazy /></Suspense>;
}
```
Each tool's `ui.tsx` calls `useWorkerOp(<factory>)` (single op) or renders `<BatchRunner>` (batch), composing the shared components above.

architecture.wasmBoundary:
## WASM Boundary Conventions + generateStaticParams

### Rust crate conventions — `rust/crates/image-tools/src/lib.rs`
- One crate per heavy domain (`image-tools`, `crypto-tools`), shared by all tools in that domain so wasm code is deduplicated. `crate-type = ["cdylib","rlib"]`.
- Boundary functions: take/return `Uint8Array` (mapped to `&[u8]`/`Vec<u8>`), primitives, and `String`. No complex serde structs across the boundary unless via `serde-wasm-bindgen` (used only for config objects, never bulk data).
- Progress via a JS callback param typed `&js_sys::Function` (or `#[wasm_bindgen] FnMut`), invoked sparingly (throttled) to avoid postMessage flooding.
- Panics: `console_error_panic_hook` set in a `#[wasm_bindgen(start)]`; a panic surfaces as a thrown JS error caught by the worker's try/catch -> `WorkerError{code:'PANIC'}`.

```rust
use wasm_bindgen::prelude::*;
use js_sys::Function;

#[wasm_bindgen(start)]
pub fn start() { console_error_panic_hook::set_once(); }

#[wasm_bindgen]
pub fn resize_image(
  bytes: &[u8], w: u32, h: u32, format: &str, quality: u8,
  on_progress: &Function,
) -> Result<Vec<u8>, JsValue> {
  let report = |r: f64, s: &str| {
    let _ = on_progress.call2(&JsValue::NULL, &JsValue::from(r), &JsValue::from(s));
  };
  report(0.0, "decoding");
  // ... image crate decode/resize/encode ...
  report(1.0, "done");
  Ok(out)
}
```

### Build conventions — `scripts/build-wasm.ts` (Bun) + `next.config.mjs`
- `bun run scripts/build-wasm.ts` runs `wasm-pack build crates/<c> --target web --out-dir ../../rust/pkg/<c> --release` for each crate. `--target web` yields ES-module bindings with an async `default()` init that `fetch()`es the `.wasm` — exactly right for dynamic `import()` inside a worker and for static export (the `.wasm` is a hashed static asset under `out/_next/static`).
- Heavy crates add `wasm-opt` (`-O3`) via wasm-pack profile.
- `next.config.mjs` enables `experiments.asyncWebAssembly` + a rule so `new Worker(new URL('./worker.ts', import.meta.url))` and the `.wasm` import are emitted as static chunks:
```js
const nextConfig = {
  output: 'export',
  images: { unoptimized: true },        // no Image Optimization server
  webpack(config) {
    config.experiments = { ...config.experiments, asyncWebAssembly: true, layers: true };
    return config;
  },
};
```
- Wasm fetched by URL means it is cached by the service worker like any other `/_next/static/*` asset -> full offline.

### Cross-origin isolation for rayon (no server)
`wasm-bindgen-rayon` needs `crossOriginIsolated === true` (COOP/COEP). With static export there's no server to set headers, so the **service worker** sets them on every navigation response (coi-serviceworker pattern):
```js
// inside public/sw.js fetch handler, for navigations:
const headers = new Headers(resp.headers);
headers.set('Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy', 'same-origin');
headers.set('Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy', 'require-corp');
```
On first load the SW registers, then reloads once to gain isolation. Non-parallel tools work without this.

### generateStaticParams — `app/tools/[slug]/page.tsx`
This is what makes every tool a prebuilt static HTML page under `output:'export'`. It enumerates all slugs from the registry at build time (the registry is generated by `prebuild` before `next build` runs).

```ts
import { getAllSlugs, getTool } from '@/lib/registry';
import { ToolHost } from '@/components/tool-host';
import type { Metadata } from 'next';
import { notFound } from 'next/navigation';

export function generateStaticParams(): { slug: string }[] {
  return getAllSlugs().map(slug => ({ slug }));   // -> out/tools/<slug>/index.html each
}

export const dynamicParams = false;               // any unknown slug => 404 at build

export function generateMetadata({ params }: { params: { slug: string } }): Metadata {
  const t = getTool(params.slug);
  return t ? { title: `${t.name} — Utility Tools`, description: t.description } : {};
}

export default function ToolPage({ params }: { params: { slug: string } }) {
  const tool = getTool(params.slug);
  if (!tool) notFound();
  return <ToolHost slug={params.slug} />;          // client component lazy-loads the tool
}
```

Build order in `package.json`:
```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "build:wasm": "bun run scripts/build-wasm.ts",
    "gen:registry": "bun run scripts/gen-registry.ts",
    "predev": "bun run build:wasm && bun run gen:registry",
    "dev": "next dev",
    "prebuild": "bun run build:wasm && bun run gen:registry",
    "build": "next build"
  }
}
```
So: wasm compiled -> registry manifest generated -> `next build` reads `getAllSlugs()` in `generateStaticParams` -> one static page per tool. Adding a tool needs no edits to routes or switches.

design.colorTokens:
OKLCH tokens. Neutrals are subtly cool/blue-tinted (hue ~250-265) — not pure gray — for a technical screen feel. Define in :root and .dark. Map all to Tailwind via hsl-less oklch().

:root (LIGHT) {
  --background: oklch(0.992 0.002 250);        /* near-white, faint cool */
  --foreground: oklch(0.205 0.012 256);        /* ink, not pure black */
  --card: oklch(1 0 0);                          /* pure white cards lift off bg */
  --card-foreground: oklch(0.205 0.012 256);
  --popover: oklch(1 0 0);
  --popover-foreground: oklch(0.205 0.012 256);
  --muted: oklch(0.967 0.004 256);             /* subtle fill, input bg, hover */
  --muted-foreground: oklch(0.486 0.014 257);  /* secondary text — passes AA on bg */
  --border: oklch(0.918 0.006 256);            /* hairline */
  --input: oklch(0.918 0.006 256);
  --ring: oklch(0.62 0.17 256);                /* focus ring = primary-ish blue */
  --primary: oklch(0.55 0.20 264);             /* indigo-blue action */
  --primary-foreground: oklch(0.985 0.002 250);
  --accent: oklch(0.955 0.012 264);            /* tinted hover/selected */
  --accent-foreground: oklch(0.30 0.10 264);
  --secondary: oklch(0.967 0.004 256);
  --secondary-foreground: oklch(0.255 0.012 256);
  --destructive: oklch(0.58 0.225 25);
  --destructive-foreground: oklch(0.985 0.002 250);
  --success: oklch(0.62 0.16 150);
  --warning: oklch(0.72 0.15 75);
  --radius: 0.5rem;
}
